onduct for  all district Town and parish councillors  within the district regardless of the  code that has been adopted as part of  the complaints process district councils  are required to appoint at least one  independent person we have to at SDC to  give an impartial view in individual  complaints to the monitoring officer the  code adopted by SDC is slightly  different from the model that we promote  for town and parishes in the district  and that's because of the differences in  size function and resources but rest  assured that the purpose of your code is  to provide a clear and ambiguous  framework in which to conduct your  public life while keeping bureaucracy to  a minimum let's now take a closer look  at the two cones  these principles of public life were  introduced by Lord Nolan who was the  first chair of the parliamentary  Committee on standards in public life  and the principles have since become  referred to as the Nolan principles they  are widely embedded across public life  and form the basis for our two codes of  conduct the leadership principle asks  you to demonstrate these principles in  your own behavior and requires you to  challenge others whenever poor behavior  occurs do take time to reflect on each  of the principles the code only operates  when you're acting in your role as a  councillor so it does not apply to your  private life that said please remember  that in your public office your behavior  is in practice always potentially under  the spotlight and the reputation of your  colleagues and your council depends on  your good conduct at all times the  behavior also under Part A of the code  are largely self-explanatory but it's  worthwhile going through them briefly  one by one number one the principle is  respect for other people disrespect runs  from the modely offensive right up to  criminal conduct such as harassment an  increasing amount of the complaints I  receive are related to the use of social  media so for further information please  see the linked document on social media  also SDC councilors need to read our  protocol on member officer relationships  which explains the role of members and  officers the need for mutual respect and  the proper channels for resolving  conflicts that is outside of the public  domain this document does not apply to  town and parish councils  to use of the councillor position for  personal financial gain as we were  seeing part two of the video  failure to declare or take action to  avoid involvement in council business  that affects your own financial  interests can constitute a criminal  offense this one is serious three using  your counselor position to improperly  confer an advantage on yourself your  family or friends or conferring a  disadvantage on someone else is another  big topic and goes back to the Noland  principles of selflessness as opposed to  selfishness why did you want to become a  counselor for if you ever find yourself  under pressure from a person or  organization that offers you some  financial benefit to make a decision in  their favor or office your reward for  acting in a certain way please contact  me immediately bribery is a very serious  criminal offense five whatever the size  of your cancel it will have some  resources such as land property staff  who work for your counsel printers paper  etc all of these resources are funded by  taxpayers they are not there for your  personal use they are not there for your  use in your political role except in  some very limited circumstances for more  information for SDC counselors please  see the resources policy again this  document does not apply to town or  parish councils six generally all  council business must be conducted  openly and transparently this includes  public access to council meetings and  the agenda reports and minutes of  meetings  however by exception information can be  withheld from public access and in those  instances where you have a general duty  not to disclose confidential information  on the other hand you must not prevent  access to information where a person has  a legitimate right of access to the  information for example under Freedom of  Information legislation 7 the last  behavioral and the one that covers all  of the other six rule  is to remember that your conduct to the  counselor will have an effect on the way  that the public see your fellow  counselors and the counsel that you  represent trust and reputation takes  years to establish but it can be all  lost in an instant planning applications  are a danger zone for counselors  especially if you're a district  counselor on our planning committee to  help you please see the link document  good practice guide to planning that  brings us to the end of part 1 of the  video in part 2 we'll be looking at  personal interests how to spot them how  to declare them and the effects of  personal interest on your counselor role  in terms of participating in counsel  business and making decisions it's now  time for you to pause the video to allow  you to take a look at the case studies  on the behavior rules that your client  will circulate so you can discuss these  with your colleagues at the end of your  discretion your Clark will circulate the  model answers once you've finished the  exercises we'll move to part 2 personal  interests  on becoming a counselor you must declare  particular matters in a public register  this helps to give the public confidence  that your work is conducted  transparently and with integrity in  areas such as planning applications for  example there are three types of  personal interest the first to which you  must declare on the public register  as closeable pecuniary interests or  DPI's for short is a narrow band of  financial interests any one of which  could be directly affected by an item of  canceled business an example would be  you submit a planning application for  your own house it will be plainly wrong  to participate in any decision to be  made on that application failure to  declare a DPI or to give false or  misleading information is a criminal  offense there's a form to complete with  some notes and your clerk will supply  you with a copy appendix a of the code  sets out the list of categories of  interests that you must declare such as  employment sponsorship and ownership of  land and property common errors I see in  completed forms are failure to give  sufficient employment details that's box  Wan and failure to give details of legal  interest in land and property box 4 so  please pay attention to the notes on  this  in the same form you must also declare  any appointments to outside bodies in  certain situations and any gifts or  hospitality received where the estimated  value is 30 pounds or more see appendix  B these last two interests are known as  other registerable interests or ori x'  the separate guidance on gifts and  hospitality if you need to see it the  form must be completed and sent to the  monitoring officer within 28 days of  your election or appointment to office  failure to do so is a criminal offense  you must also give details for your  spouse or civil partner equivalent  although these details are shown as only  your interests on the public register  it's equally important to keep your  register of interests up to date this is  not just a protocol or good practice it  is a legal requirement again you must  notify the monitoring officer within 28  days of any change if you have any  questions when filling in the form  please seek further advice from your  clerk or from the monitoring officer  circumstances can change and what might  have been irrelevant to declare upon  your appointment may become relevant  over time with DPI's the law is very  clear if you attend a council meeting as  a nice one of business to be discussed  directly effects one or more of your  declared DPI's then you must not  participate which means that you must  not speak or vote at the meeting