ning  application on land that she owns you  would have an obvious bias there's a  two-step test to help you decide whether  you can participate in a matter step  one does the matter affect the interest  more than it affects the majority of  people in the ward or parish if the  answer is yes you then ask would a  reasonable member of the public knowing  all the facts believe that it would  affect your view the wider public  interest situations can arise where a  declaration of interest is not required  under the code but you still need to  make sure that you are not biased and  that you don't have a fixed position on  a decision to be made and retain a  closed mind known as a predetermination  right up to the point when you make a  decision otherwise the decision that's  at risk have been challenged possibly in  the courts in general the rule against  bias looks to the appearance or risk of  bias rather than bias in fact in order  to ensure that justice should not only  be done but should be seen to be done  obviously where bias or predetermination  arises you should not participate in the  decision an example of bias would occur  if you're involved in recruiting a new  employee to your council and you held a  personal grudge against one of the  applicants for some reason then clearly  you should not be helping to make the  appointment decision planning  applications also provide fertile ground  for predetermination so if you have very  strong views about an application and  you know that whatever happens your view  will not change when you have to make a  decision and this includes giving views  to the local planning authority then  that is predetermination the Localism  Act 2011 makes clear that you are not  taken to be predetermined when you make  a decision just because you have  previously done anything that directly  or indirectly indicated what view you  may take in relation to a matter and  that matter was relevant to the decision  in other words expressing 